\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Hermitian generators in the complexification}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{The proposition uses the real Lie algebra of the compact group,
with bracket #{[X,Y]=XY-YX} and skew-adjoint elements. The physics
normalization lives in the complexification. The map is #{X\mapsto iX}.
Set #{T=iX}. Then #{T^{\dagger}=T}, and the link of \ref{fdip-007F} is
#{\exp(aX)=\exp(-iaT)}. The Hermitian field of the older
normalization is #{A=-iX=-T}, and its curvature
#{\partial_jA_k-\partial_kA_j-i[A_j,A_k]} equals #{-iF_{jk}}.
If the real bracket is #{[X_a,X_b]=f_{ab}{}^cX_c}, the same constants
in the complexification satisfy #{[T_a,T_b]=if_{ab}{}^cT_c}.
That equation is the bracket of the complexification.}

\p{Suppose the Hermitian generators in the chosen representation satisfy
##{\trace(T_\alpha T_\gamma)
 =T(\rho)\delta_{\alpha\gamma},\qquad T(\rho)>0.}
For the skew-adjoint generators #{X_\alpha=-iT_\alpha} the same
constant is the real-algebra form,
##{-\trace(X_\alpha X_\gamma)=T(\rho)\delta_{\alpha\gamma}.}
Write #{F_{jk}=\sum_\alpha F_{jk}^\alpha X_\alpha} and fix #{g>0}.
Choosing
##{\beta=\frac{r a^{d-4}}{T(\rho)g^2}}
gives the classical limit
##{\begin{aligned}
S_W(a)&=\frac1{2g^2}\int
 \sum_{j<k,\alpha}(F_{jk}^\alpha)^2\,dx+O(a)\\
 &=\frac1{4g^2}\int
 \sum_{j,k,\alpha}(F_{jk}^\alpha)^2\,dx+O(a).
\end{aligned}}
The second sum counts both orders, with #{F_{kj}=-F_{jk}} and
#{F_{jj}=0}. The trace normalization is an explicit assumption;
it must not be imposed on directions annihilated by a nonfaithful
representation. For the fundamental representation of #{SU(r)}
with #{T(\rho)=1/2}, this is #{\beta=2r a^{d-4}/g^2}.
For unit-charge #{U(1)}, #{r=T(\rho)=1}, giving
#{\beta=a^{d-4}/g^2}.}

\p{Positive-definiteness of #{\langle Y,Z\rangle=-\trace(YZ)}
is a property of the compact real form. On the split real form
#{\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb R)} the same coordinate trace is indefinite,
so the quadratic density above does not remain a positive action
density. The normalized Haar probability used by the compact gauge
section is likewise a compactness choice; \ref{fdip-00LY} names the
split form that has no such probability.}
